<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=kerosene">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerosene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Kerosine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kerosene, also called paraffin, is a combustible hydrocarbon liquid distilled from petroleum and used as a fuel for lamps, stoves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">jet engines</a>; the distilling of crude oil into kerosene was first written about in the 9th century by the Persian scholar Rāzi. The modern industry began with lamp oil from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> Gesner coined the name for a fuel he claimed to have demonstrated in 1846, and James Young patented his paraffin oil in 1850. After Edwin Drake's well of 1859, American refiners switched to petroleum, and kerosene largely replaced whale oil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerosene.jpg" alt="Kerosene" width="320" height="548" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R. Henrik Nilsson · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4_litre_Kristallfotogen_kerosene_can_by_Aktiebolaget_Lyse_Sweden_early_20th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Distilling petroleum into kerosene was first written about in the ninth century by the Persian scholar Rāzi, whose Kitab al-Asrar describes two methods, using an alembic, for making what he called naft abyad, white naphtha. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/distillation">Distillation</a> was repeated until the product was clear and safe to burn. Kerosene was also made at the time from oil shale and bitumen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> oil was known to chemists by the 1700s as a byproduct of coal gas and tar, but it burned smokily, so the market for indoor lamps outside the cities went to whale oil, especially that of sperm whales.</p><p>The Canadian geologist Abraham Pineo Gesner claimed to have shown in 1846, in Charlottetown, a process of distilling a clear lamp fuel from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>. He coined the name kerosene from the Greek roots for wax and oil. In 1848 the Scottish chemist James Young began distilling coal, especially torbanite, and named one liquid paraffine oil because it congealed like paraffin wax. He patented his process in 1850 and built the first truly commercial oil works at Bathgate in 1851. Gesner moved to Long Island in 1854, obtained his patent that year and registered Kerosene as a trademark.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1851 Samuel Martin Kier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> began selling lamp oil to miners, distilled from the crude oil that fouled his salt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>. The Polish pharmacist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignacy-ukasiewicz">Ignacy Łukasiewicz</a> showed his lamps during an emergency operation on 31 July 1853 and set up a refinery near Jasło in 1859. Edwin Drake's well in western Pennsylvania in 1859 let refiners side-step the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-oil patents, and by the 1860s the American illuminating oil industry had switched to petroleum. The name lost its proprietary status and became the generic kerosene.</p><p>Kerosene was a fire risk: in 1880 nearly two of every five fires in New York City were caused by defective kerosene lamps. The American whaling fleet peaked at 199 ships in 1858, and only 39 sailed in 1876. Electric lighting displaced kerosene in cities from the late 19th century, and in 1909 motor fuels overtook it as the main use of refined petroleum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It stayed in use in stoves, heaters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractors</a> and, in highly refined form, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">jet engines</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Kerosene is a low-viscosity, clear liquid of hydrocarbons with 6 to 20 carbon atoms per molecule, taken by fractional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/distillation">distillation</a> of petroleum between 150 and 275 °C. It is called paraffin in the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and the Primus stove of 1892 burns it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kerosene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>9th century</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Persia</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Rāzi · <a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The modern industry began with lamp oil from coal: Abraham Gesner claimed priority from 1846 and James Young patented his process in 1850.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/distillation">Distillation<span>c. 1200 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">Oil well<span>347 CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=kerosene">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
